Not 'we searched 720 settings' -- named cases, each its own run,
each printing its own result, so any single line can be checked.


  PART 1  --  building the fix, one change at a time
  Full load, 100 V and 10 A. The device switches on at 1.4 V, so the
  gate of the device that is meant to be OFF has to stay under that.

  case                               peak gate     margin       loss   verdict
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  1. Fastest drive, nothing else       +1.649 V    -0.249 V    4.018 uJ   FALSE TURN-ON
      8 pull-up slices, no clamp, off gate at 0 V
  2. Turn the Miller clamp on          +0.830 V    +0.570 V    4.052 uJ   safe
      same drive; the clamp shorts the off gate down
  3. Add the -2 V off-bias rail        -1.176 V    +2.576 V    5.230 uJ   safe
      same again; the off gate now sits at -2 V, not 0 V
  4. Slow the drive right down         -1.674 V    +3.074 V    7.908 uJ   safe
      1 pull-up slice instead of 8 -- the safe-but-wasteful extreme
  5. The setting the search chose      +1.059 V    +0.341 V    3.992 uJ   safe
      8/8/1 slices, 25 ns dead time, clamp on, 0 V rail

  PART 2  --  does one fixed setting fit every operating point?
  Case 5's driver, unchanged, at two operating points, sweeping only
  the dead time. If the cheapest dead time were the same number at
  both, there would be nothing to adapt.

